Z||000002||DXR_DAD||12-09-2000||12:53 PM||||I just looked and it just gives a stock no.<BR>for the lowering springs = 55ED5286U Then you <BR>state the type, year, make, model, Front or Rear.  $79.99 per set (2)<P><BR>DXR_DAD ||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||12-09-2000||01:00 PM||||just a suggestion... <P>if you order the Eibach's from Carparts and use the 33% off coupon (on my thread about suspension rebuid), they will cost you just a few dollars more than the ones from JC Whitney!<P>I bought mine for about $212 delivered!<P><BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bob Englert||12-09-2000||01:39 PM||||I would never use the Spring lowering kit in the pciture above on the car but they work great to hold the stock springs compressed enough to get them off and back on the car when doing front suspension work.  They clear the control arms where normal springs compressors don't fit.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000005||PlasticWrapd||12-09-2000||01:48 PM||||I wouldn't recomment using the "spring compressor" to lower the car.  Normal suspension action causes the compressors to rub and wear away at the spring.  There always seems to be clearance problems and they rub on everything, especially on the front.  They are designed to pull two of the coils together and hold them against each other.  Looking at the distance and the number of coils on the rear springs of my 84, this would drop the car 2.5-3" and cause the wheels to rub.  <B>Use 'em at yer own risk</B>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||12-09-2000||04:06 PM||||the JCW springs are made by Suspension Techniques.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000007||007DOUG||12-09-2000||04:41 PM||||Zorba: Noticed your blue springs.<P>Are those 2 1/2" lowering springs?<P>I had those before. Beat me (and my fiero) to death. But damn did they make the car look sweet.<P>------------------<BR>5 Indy pace Cars<BR>84 T-top<BR>84 SE<BR>86 SE V6 153K<BR>86 GT<BR>86 GT now with a 4:10<BR>86 GT 3.4 Pearl White T-top<BR>2 88 GT's (twins)<P>However......<BR><I>"Time is my most precious and most perishable of all my possessions."</I>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Zorba||12-09-2000||04:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 007DOUG:<BR><B>Zorba: Noticed your blue springs.<P>Are those 2 1/2" lowering springs?<P> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>They are but they aren't mine. The only reason I would use lowering springs in front is to deal with a 2" drop on my rear suspension with the new v8 in there. I have temporarily solved this problem with a set of 2 1/4 inch rubber risers that go in-between the strut coils.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||12-09-2000||07:28 PM||||i tried to get the susp. tech. front springs from jc whitney last spring. they backordered them once and eventually canceled the order themselves. told me to try again in a few months but i got some off ebay.||||reg||1
Z||000010||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||08:38 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zorba:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by 007DOUG:<BR>[b]Zorba: Noticed your blue springs.<P>Are those 2 1/2" lowering springs?<P> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>They are but they aren't mine. The only reason I would use lowering springs in front is to deal with a 2" drop on my rear suspension with the new v8 in there. I have temporarily solved this problem with a set of 2 1/4 inch rubber risers that go in-between the strut coils.<BR>[/B][/QUOTE]<P>insted of lowering the front raise the rear with a  coilover conversion ryane motor sports sells such a kit and it is less than a good set of springs and you should be able to install them yourself. <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ryanemotorsports.com</A>       <p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000011||Oreif||12-09-2000||11:34 PM||||Ryane Motorsports no longer makes parts for Fiero's. The have sold all the Fiero Items.<BR>Below is a quote from the Ryane website. The new owners do not have their website up as of yet. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> <B> We are happy to announce that Ryane Motorsports has sold the exclusive manufacturing rights, tooling, jigs and production capability to continue supplying Fiero based products to the Fiero and Kit Car industry. <P>The new owners, Mike and Dave Held will be manufacturing products under the Held Motorsports name. Mike and Dave bring complementary experience and talent and we are convinced that they will continue supplying high quality parts to the market. Held Motorsports has retained Ryane Motorsports' consulting expertise to assist in the transition as well as future prototyping and development projects. <P></B> <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Archie||12-10-2000||10:26 AM||||Mario, I think you are making a mistake here.<P>I think I recall you saying that your donor car for this V-8 swap was a high mileage car that was not running when you got it.<P>If, in fact, you are down 2" in the rear ride height ... It is my opinion that the rear suspension on that car is worn out. Shocks, springs and "A" arm bushings are what I suspect is the problem.<P>I've done more than a few V-8 swaps and I've never had a car that lost that much ride height with the addition of a V-8. Over the years I've measured before and after ride height on cars I've converted and the most ride height lowering I've seen is 3/8". If you have more than that, then you need to be fixing the problems with the rear suspension before you modify the front.<P>I know that RCC and Ryane have been telling people that they should add bigger springs etc. as part of a V-8 swap, but I disagree with that. Neither one of those guys has ever built a stock Fiero on a stock suspension into a V-8 Fiero on a stock suspension. Their statements that you need bigger springs etc. on a V-8 car is not based on Their experiences but rather on their opinion.<P>I know that people are going to flame me for that statement. So, just hang on before you get out the flame throwers. I've used the Ryane and RCC stuff on some of my cars and some customer cars and I've liked it fine. Sometimes those parts were used to lower the front and rear after a V-8 swap, but, they have never been used to raise a V-8 car out of the dirt. If you look at my custmer car page <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/customer.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/customer.htm</A>  You'll see several Fieros that were riding on stock suspensions, and they are still looking like they need to be lowered in the rear.<P>Now, any of you guys can prove this to yourself. If you have a stock Fiero out in the driveway and let's say you weigh 200 pounds.... Go out and sit on the rear bumper, if the car goes down 2", I'll buy you lunch.<P>In conclusion, Don't lower the front to match the back..... find out what's wrong in the rear and fix it.<P>Archie<P>Everyone of these cars were riding on stock suspensions with V-8 engines when the pics were taken.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/morme.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/morme.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/dburns.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/dburns.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/bolle.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/bolle.htm</A> <P>Enough said..... fix the problem, don't make more problems by not addressing the real deal.<P>Archie<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 12-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||12-10-2000||10:55 AM||||The spring rates on a base coupe are 220lbs per inch and on an SE/GT/coupe with WS6 suspension the spring rates are 250lbs per inch. Which means that in order to drop the back of the car 2 inches on a base coupe the engine would have to be 440 lbs heavier than the stock engine.<BR>I think there is only a 125lbs difference between 4cyl and V-8 (which would drop car by 1/2" technically) and slightly less than a 60lbs difference from a V-6 to a V-8. (about 3/8" average) If the vehicle has Eibach or Suspension Tech lowering springs, their spring rates are 325lbs per inch.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||bubbajoexx||12-10-2000||12:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>Mario, I think you are making a mistake here.<P><BR>I think I recall you saying that your donor car for this V-8 swap was a high mileage car that was not running when you got it.<P><BR>If, in fact, you are down 2" in the rear ride height ... It is my opinion that the rear suspension on that car is worn out. Shocks, springs and "A" arm bushings are what I suspect is the problem.<P><BR>I've done more than a few V-8 swaps and I've never had a car that lost that much ride height with the addition of a V-8. Over the years I've measured before and after ride height on cars I've converted and the most ride height lowering I've seen is 3/8". If you have more than that, then you need to be fixing the problems with the rear suspension before you modify the front.<P><BR>I know that RCC and Ryane have been telling people that they should add bigger springs etc. as part of a V-8 swap, but I disagree with that. Neither one of those guys has ever built a stock Fiero on a stock suspension into a V-8 Fiero on a stock suspension. Their statements that you need bigger springs etc. on a V-8 car is not based on Their experiences but rather on their opinion.<P><BR>I know that people are going to flame me for that statement. So, just hang on before you get out the flame throwers. I've used the Ryane and RCC stuff on some of my cars and some customer cars and I've liked it fine. Sometimes those parts were used to lower the front and rear after a V-8 swap, but, they have never been used to raise a V-8 car out of the dirt. If you look at my custmer car page <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/customer.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/customer.htm</A>  You'll see several Fieros that were riding on stock suspensions, and they are still looking like they need to be lowered in the rear.<P><BR>Now, any of you guys can prove this to yourself. If you have a stock Fiero out in the driveway and let's say you weigh 200 pounds.... Go out and sit on the rear bumper, if the car goes down 2", I'll buy you lunch.<P><BR>In conclusion, Don't lower the front to match the back..... find out what's wrong in the rear and fix it.<P><BR>Archie<P><BR>Everyone of these cars were riding on stock suspensions with V-8 engines when the pics were taken.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/morme.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/morme.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/dburns.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/dburns.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/bolle.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/bolle.htm</A> <P><BR>Enough said..... fix the problem, don't make more problems by not addressing the real deal.<P><BR>Archie<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 12-10-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree with you totaly the stock fiero suspension   is well capable to handel the extra weight of the small block chevy wth out modification.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Archie||12-10-2000||01:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bubbajoexx:<BR><B> .....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow, If I had known you were going to quote everything I said, I wouldn't have said so much.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000016||Zorba||12-10-2000||05:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> High mileage...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, I never thought you guys would respond to my dilema but thank you. The car had 76,000 miles and it was a running car with a running I-4 in it. The ride was very smooth. In fact I enjoyed driving it very much except for the lack of engine power. I will have to drive it a bit more to see if anything is bottoming out but I still have quite a bit of spring travel in the rear.<BR>IF I need to replace struts (even though they aren't leaking) and the coil springs, what do you suggest? Rear springs for a GT?<BR>I am sure that If I get a higher spring rate, the sagging might go away. Maybe I made it sound worse than it really is.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Archie||12-10-2000||07:23 PM||||Well Mario, whatever you've got, there is no way in heck that the car should have lost 2" in ride height by just installing a V-8.<P>I'm sure you've seen pics. of my Blue Chop Top car. That car was lowered (via lowering springs) 1.5" and If you look closely at that car it still looks like the rear of the car sits a bit high.<P>Archie<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3-11-5.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
